-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell D23 (S1-NS (a), DEFINER twin of I19): this module DEFINES its own
-- `size` and reaches the interface ONLY by naming a SIBLING method. `import ifc.{tag}`
-- admits `tag` and nothing else, so `size` is not in S1's right operand here, it is not a
-- shadow, and the module's OWN top-level binding answers: 42.
--
-- 🚨 THE DEFINER S0 THE S1-NS PASS NAMES, AND IT HAD NO FIXTURE AT ALL. Under per-ROW
-- admission the interface was admitted because a DIFFERENT method was named, `size` became a
-- shadow, and S2's definer inversion never got the chance to fire -- so a module's own
-- top-level function was silently replaced by a foreign impl body, printing 777 at exit 0
-- with no diagnostic on any verb. A module losing its own definition to an interface it
-- never named is the worst shape in this corpus, and the corpus could not express it.
--
-- ⚠️ SCOPE, measured rather than assumed: `main` ALREADY gives 42 here. This cell fails only
-- against the intermediate state of the PR that introduced per-ROW admission, so it guards a
-- regression that unit introduced rather than one it inherited. It is kept because the shape
-- is the one the ruling is written about and nothing else in the corpus expresses it -- but
-- do not cite it as evidence that the unit changed behaviour on `main`.
--
-- The receiver is String, inside the standalone's domain AND at the impl's head, so both
-- candidates typecheck: only a pinned VALUE can tell 42 from 777. The definer/importer pair
-- matters because S2 treats the two kinds differently -- I19 is the importer half, and a fix
-- that repaired one without the other would leave this one silent.
import ifc.{tag}

size : String -> Int
size s = 42

main = println (size "abc")
